    "message": "fix(routing): anchor the commit boundary to content, not to the first frame\n\nThe provider holds a brief window of SSE so an early cutoff can be retried\ninvisibly. That window was anchored to the first frame of *any* kind, so a\nmodel that emitted `message_start` and then stalled had already committed the\nroute: nothing had reached the reader, and yet no fallback was possible,\nbecause a second attempt would put a second `message_start` into a stream the\nclient was already reading.\n\nMeasured on 21 days of the author's live traffic (150,846 requests), the 500\nrequests that ran the full 600s budget with a chain configured split cleanly:\n\n    393  non-streaming, scaffolding only   reader saw nothing\n    106  streaming, real content produced  reader saw output\n      1  streaming, scaffolding only\n\nOnly the first and last groups are recoverable at all, and both were lost to\nthis anchor. The 106 have genuinely shown the reader something and are out of\nreach of any fallback; they need a stall guard, which is not this change.\n\nThe window now starts at the first non-scaffolding frame. Scaffolding --\nmessage_start, message_delta, message_stop, content_block_start,\ncontent_block_stop, ping -- never starts it, so a stream that produces only\nenvelope stays uncommitted however long it takes.\n\nThe window itself is kept rather than committing on content's first byte.\nThat distinction is the whole point of the buffer: bytes still held have not\nreached the client, which is what lets the provider retry an immediate cutoff\nwith no visible seam. Committing on first content removed that grace and broke\ntwo existing tests, which is how the trade-off was found.\n\nTwo-way, not three-way. An unrecognised frame is treated exactly like content:\nit starts the window, so unfamiliar output is delayed by 0.75s rather than\nheld indefinitely. An earlier draft had a third \"unknown\" class; mutation\ntesting showed it changed nothing any caller did.\n\n`message_delta` is scaffolding despite carrying a `delta` key -- that delta\nholds a stop reason and a usage count, not any part of the answer.\nClassifying on frame type rather than on the presence of a `delta` field is\nwhat keeps that right; an earlier draft got it wrong and mutation testing\nfound it as dead-and-harmful code.\n\nVerification\n------------\nGuards mutation-tested, each failing by name:\n\n    M1 scaffolding starts the window again      -> scaffolding_never_commits\n    M2 content commits instantly                -> 12 tests incl. both provider\n                                                   invisible-retry contracts\n    M3 content_block_delta treated as envelope  -> 5 tests\n    M5 byte cap removed                         -> byte_cap_still_bounds\n\nM3 survived two earlier drafts and the code was changed rather than the test:\nfirst because \"content\" and \"unknown\" behaved identically, then because the\n`delta`-presence check was redundant for content_block_delta and actively\nwrong for message_delta.\n\nThree existing tests were updated, not silenced: the behaviour they pinned\nchanged deliberately, and each now states the new anchor.\n\nLocal CI: ruff format --check, ruff check, ty clean; pytest 4,811 passed with\nthe documented 6 test_install_ps1_* baseline failures.\n\nNot done\n--------\nThis is NOT demonstrated to fix the 393 production hangs. A stub upstream\nemitting a role-only delta, and then one emitting empty content deltas, both\nfell back correctly on unmodified main as well -- so neither reproduces\nwhatever frame flushed the buffer in production, and the fix cannot be\ncredited with them on this evidence. What bounds those regardless of cause is\nthe per-attempt deadline, which is the next change in this series.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
